<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mirza Kuchik Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1880">1880</a>–<a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Kuchik Khan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mirza Kuchik Khan was an Iranian revolutionary who from 1915 led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jangal-movement">Jangali movement</a> in the forests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> against foreign intervention and the weak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> government. In 1920 he allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> to found the Persian Socialist Soviet Republic. After the alliance broke down he fled government forces into the mountains, where he died in 1921.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mirza-kuchik-khan.jpg" alt="Mirza Kuchik Khan" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mirza_Kochak_Khan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born Yunes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasht">Rasht</a>, the son of a merchant from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> known as Mirza Bozorg, and so was called Mirza Kuchak, the younger. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-ali-shah-qajar">Mohammad Ali Shah Qajar</a> shut down the Iranian Parliament in a coup in June 1908, uprisings followed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> and Rasht. Kuchik Khan tried to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sattar-khan">Sattar Khan</a>'s forces in Tabriz but was prevented by illness, and he was wounded in the Constitutionalist war and travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> for treatment. In July 1909 he was one of the lower-ranking commanders of the forces that took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> from the north.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The old privileged class soon regained control of the new regime, the revolutionaries were disarmed, and Russian and British interference deepened the unrest. With the Society of Islamic Union, Kuchik Khan began a rising in the forests of the southern Caspian, first based at Kasma, which drew on the new national <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a> and the peasants. The Jangalis defeated local government and Russian troops, but at Manjil on 12 June 1918 they were beaten by British and White Russian forces under General Dunsterville and Colonel Lazar Bicherakhov, who used artillery, armoured cars and aircraft.</p><p>In May 1920 a Soviet fleet under Fyodor Raskolnikov entered the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandar-e-anzali">Anzali</a>. Kuchik Khan agreed to cooperate on condition that a Socialist Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> be proclaimed under his leadership without Soviet interference, but disputes with the Soviets and the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> soon turned bloody, and a petition he sent to Lenin settled nothing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1921, after an agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, the Soviets withdrew their support, and government forces under Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Khan</a> overran the republic. Kuchik Khan and a companion, Gaouk, died of frostbite in the Talesh mountains. A local landlord cut off his head, which was shown in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasht">Rasht</a> and sent to Reza Khan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>; friends returned it to his tomb in Rasht during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Historians such as Gregory Yeghikian and Ebrahim Fakhrayi trace the movement's fall to the communists' extremism and to his own cautious religious views.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mirza Kuchik Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1915 <b>Mirza Kuchik Khan</b> led the Jangali ('forest') movement in Gilan against foreign intervention and the weak Qajar government.</blockquote><a href="/state/persian-socialist-soviet-republic">Persian Socialist Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasht">Rasht</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in Khalkhal County, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.28,49.59&amp;year=1906" aria-label="Rasht on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mirza Kuchik Khan"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4295 4347h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4295 4347h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.28,49.59&amp;year=1906">What was here in 1906? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
